---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import MetricsLayerDiagram from '../../components/MetricsLayerDiagram.astro';
import Sidenote from '../../components/Sidenote.astro';
---

<Base
  title="A tested metrics layer for four social platforms — Shangwei Yang"
  description="Rebuilding a newsroom's social reporting in dbt on BigQuery: one set of definitions across four platforms, 13 legacy views replaced with zero dashboard changes, and CI/CD around all of it."
>
  <article class="prose has-margin">
    <p class="eyebrow">Case study · 2026 · TaiwanPlus</p>
    <h1>A tested metrics layer for four social platforms</h1>
    <p class="meta">
      dbt · BigQuery · GitHub Actions · Python ·
      <a href="https://github.com/shangweiyang79/taiwanplus-dbt">github repo</a>
    </p>

    <p class="lede">
      Leadership at TaiwanPlus kept asking how the News Department was doing on social, and nobody
      could give one answer. Three units publish on YouTube, Instagram, Facebook, and X. Each
      platform counts a view differently, the old reporting views covered only one unit, and live
      dashboards depended on those views staying exactly as they were. With no data engineer on
      staff, I rebuilt the layer in dbt and switched production without changing a single dashboard.
    </p>

    <figure>
      <MetricsLayerDiagram />
      <figcaption>
        <span class="fig-no">Fig. 1</span>Extract and load stay outside dbt, in scheduled Python
        jobs; the raw BigQuery tables are the contract between the two sides. Dashboards read
        compatibility views, while the assistant, forecasts, and biweekly report share the certified
        definitions.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Two numbers, both called views</h2>
    <p>
      Two reports gave different September views for the same account, and for one unit the gap was
      about 2×. Neither was wrong. One summed the views that happened in September; the other summed
      the lifetime views of videos published in September. I modeled them as separate fact tables
      with different metric names, wrote down which question each one answers, and certified daily
      traffic as the KPI.<Sidenote id="grains">
        "How are we doing" goes to daily traffic, "which videos" to video level, and "why did it
        change" to both, clearly labeled. The AI assistant gets the same rule, and eval questions
        check that it picks the right grain.
      </Sidenote>
    </p>

    <h2>Never sum a snapshot table</h2>
    <p>
      Instagram stores one cumulative snapshot per reel per day, so a plain <code>SUM</code>
      inflated views 100× or more. The legacy daily report took today's account total minus
      yesterday's, which went negative whenever the API dropped reels from its response. Now each
      reel is compared with its own previous snapshot, and cumulative values take a running max so a
      missing reading can't create a negative. A test fails if a negative day ever comes back.
    </p>

    <h2>A definition change is not growth</h2>
    <p>
      In August 2026 YouTube relaxed its view threshold, and Views jumped overnight. A chart alone
      would have called that growth. Views stayed the KPI, because it is what YouTube Studio shows,
      and I added engaged views as a reference metric at both grains. Instagram's method change is
      recorded the same way, in a column that travels with the data, so a reader can separate real
      movement from a redefinition.
    </p>

    <h2>Moving live dashboards without touching them</h2>
    <p>
      Thirteen hand-written BigQuery views sat behind live Looker Studio dashboards, so a broken
      migration would have been visible to leadership immediately. I rebuilt each view in dbt under
      the same name with the same columns, then wrote a script that diffs every view row by row:
      exact on non-float columns, 1e-6 relative tolerance on floats.
    </p>
    <p>
      All 13 reconciled. The only differences were floating-point noise below 1e-11 and 2 rows of an
      approximate median.<Sidenote id="approx">
        Those two rows also change when the old view's own SQL is rerun, so they are a property of
        <code>APPROX_QUANTILES</code>, not of the migration.
      </Sidenote> The cutover needed zero dashboard changes.
    </p>

    <h2>What keeps it honest now</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Layer</th><th>What runs</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Tests</td><td>40 dbt tests: 30 generic and 10 singular, such as "the fact table equals an independent sum of the raw sources"</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Freshness</td><td>Thresholds tuned to how each source group actually loads</td></tr>
        <tr><td>CI</td><td>Every pull request builds the whole project in an isolated dataset and runs every test</td></tr>
        <tr><td>CD</td><td>Merging to <code>main</code> deploys production, keyless through Workload Identity Federation</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Daily checks</td><td>A read-only job runs freshness and tests against production and emails on failure</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Documentation</td><td>Definitions live in one file and are written into BigQuery column descriptions</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>One definition, read by an LLM too</h2>
    <p>
      The semantic layer holds 17 metrics across 2 semantic models. A script generates the AI
      context directly from the dbt manifest, so that file is never edited by hand and can't drift
      from the models. The read-only assistant built on it passes all 20 golden questions in its
      deployed mode, including traps like ranking platforms on absolute views or picking the wrong
      grain.
    </p>

    <h2>What I haven't built</h2>
    <p>
      Every model is a view today, so hot marts are the next thing to materialize. Slim CI isn't
      worth the upkeep while full builds stay fast. Snapshots for slowly changing mappings, such as
      which unit owns a channel, are also still ahead.
    </p>

    <h2>What this project demonstrates</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Modeling the question, not just the data</strong> — the hard part was deciding what "views" should mean, then encoding that in table and metric names.</li>
      <li><strong>Migrations you can prove</strong> — same names, row-level reconciliation, and no change for the people reading the dashboards.</li>
      <li><strong>Software practice for data</strong> — tests, CI/CD, keyless deploys, and daily checks around a warehouse project.</li>
      <li><strong>One source for every reader</strong> — dashboards, reports, forecasts, and an LLM all trace back to the same definitions.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="meta">
      Models, tests, and CI/CD:
      <a href="https://github.com/shangweiyang79/taiwanplus-dbt">shangweiyang79/taiwanplus-dbt</a>.
      The TaiwanPlus data is proprietary and not included; results are given as relative figures.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

